WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.690 --> 00:00:19.279
During the Bolshevik revolution, the Romanov
dynasty was killed after over a hundred-year

00:00:19.279 --> 00:00:20.700
reign in Russia.

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:25.070
The bodies of the parents and all five children
were laid on the ground.

00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:29.230
But when the corpses were later moved and
given a proper burial, the bodies of the son,

00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:32.540
Alexei, and the princess Anastasia were missing.

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:36.570
This gave the Russian people hope that at
least two of these innocent children managed

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:39.470
to play dead long enough to escape.

00:00:39.470 --> 00:00:43.230
Several years later, a young woman was admitted
to a mental hospital in Germany.

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:47.780
There were scars all over her body, and she
clearly suffered some traumatic event, but

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:50.079
her mind had blocked out the painful memories.

00:00:50.079 --> 00:00:52.559
She couldn’t even remember her own name.

00:00:52.559 --> 00:00:57.550
The only clue about where she came from was
the fact that she spoke with a Russian accent.

00:00:57.550 --> 00:01:01.999
Months later, a Russian patient was admitted
to that same hospital, and she immediately

00:01:01.999 --> 00:01:04.460
began to kneel in front of this young girl.

00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:08.470
She claimed that she recognized her as the
missing Princess Anastasia.

00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:10.460
But who was this young woman?

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:15.330
Was she really the long-lost princess, or
would this go down as one of the biggest frauds

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:19.810
in history?

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:31.550
The Young Life of Anastasia
Anastasia Romanov was born in 1901 to Tsar

00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:34.990
Nicholas II, and his wife, Tsarina Alexandra
Feodorovna.

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:39.600
Her proper title was “Grand Duchess”,
but no one ever addressed the young girl with

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:41.980
this proper title in her daily life.

00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:45.170
She had three older sisters- Olga, Maria,
and Tatiana.

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:48.020
Her younger brother, Alexei, was meant to
ascend the throne someday.

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:52.420
The Romanov children were the first royals
to have such an extensive collection of family

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:53.420
photographs.

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:57.840
There are hundreds of pictures and videos
of their family vacations, and playing games

00:01:57.840 --> 00:01:59.230
on the palace grounds.

00:01:59.230 --> 00:02:02.010
It was easy to see that they lived a carefree
life.

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:06.710
Their pictures were publicized all over Russia,
and they were became the darlings of the country.

00:02:06.710 --> 00:02:10.950
Anastasia was an extremely energetic toddler
who had a bubbly personality.

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:14.720
She was very intelligent, so she grew bored
of her tutor’s lessons very quickly.

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:19.569
She was a tomboy, and she enjoyed playing
outside far more than doing lady-like activities

00:02:19.569 --> 00:02:20.780
with her sisters.

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:25.750
She was described as clumsy, and she was always
climbing on something or running around.

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:28.580
She seemed to have an endless amount of energy.

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:33.770
According to some of her cousins and ladies-in-waiting,
Anastasia’s boredom manifested in some terrible

00:02:33.770 --> 00:02:34.770
ways.

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:38.040
She would entertainment herself by playing
pranks on people, and her cousins called her

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:41.010
“evil”, and one of the servants called
her a “terrorist”.

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:45.200
She tripped her servants in the hallway, climbed
up trees and refusing to come down, and hid

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:47.050
rocks inside of snowballs.

00:02:47.050 --> 00:02:51.340
She would often cheat during games, and she
lied a lot to get herself out of trouble.

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:57.000
At one point, a monk named Grigori Rasputin
became the spiritual advisor of the Romanov

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:58.000
family.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:02.360
Tsarina Alexandra wholeheartedly believed
that he had magical powers that healed her

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:04.990
son, Alexei, who was suffering from hemophilia.

00:03:04.990 --> 00:03:08.540
Rasputin was able to heal him on the brink
of death.

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:13.069
Because of this, she listened to his advice,
and gave him free reign in the royal palace.

00:03:13.069 --> 00:03:17.620
Anastasia may or may not have realized it,
but her father, Nicholas, had a problem with

00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:18.620
drugs.

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:22.030
In one of his diary entries, he wrote about
waking up with a stuffy nose, and planning

00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:24.470
to fix it by snorting cocaine.

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:28.520
Back then, cocaine was actually prescribed
by doctors as a medication, so it didn’t

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:31.040
hold the stigma that it does today.

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:35.630
While she did not take recreational drugs,
Tsarina Alexandra was described as being anxious

00:03:35.630 --> 00:03:37.110
and high-strung.

00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:41.600
Anastasia commented that her mother barely
ever indulged in coffee or wine, because she

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:42.750
had a bad stomach.

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:47.040
She was also incredibly spiritual, and often
went to mystics to help her make decisions

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:48.690
about her day-to-day life.

00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:54.190
Rasputin was also known for using morphine,
cocaine, and opium on a regular basis.

00:03:54.190 --> 00:03:58.319
Some historians believe that the closer he
got to the Tsar and Tsarina, the more the

00:03:58.319 --> 00:04:04.370
couple became dependent on the drugs and guidance
through fanatical religion that Rasputin was

00:04:04.370 --> 00:04:05.910
introducing them to.

00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:09.879
After witnessing the terrible things that
drugs did to the adults in her life, Anastasia

00:04:09.879 --> 00:04:13.450
became obsessed with clean eating and natural
remedies.

00:04:13.450 --> 00:04:16.510
She spoke against drug use, and became a vegetarian.

00:04:16.510 --> 00:04:20.510
Every time she was sick, she would throw a
fit, refusing to take any medicine, because

00:04:20.510 --> 00:04:24.280
she didn’t want to be like Rasputin and
her father.

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:28.190
Servants noted that Rasputin was given a little
too much access to the children.

00:04:28.190 --> 00:04:30.540
In 1910, one of the nannies, a woman named
Sofia Ivanovna Tyutcheva, was particularly

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:35.440
disturbed about the fact that the monk often
witnessed the four girls when they were in

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:36.440
their nightgowns.

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:39.380
He had the freedom to enter the children’s
nursery whenever he wanted.

00:04:39.380 --> 00:04:43.020
That same year, one of the other governesses,
Maria Ivanovna Vishnyakova, claimed that Rasputin

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:44.020
raped her.

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:45.190
She tried to tell the Tsarina.

00:04:45.190 --> 00:04:50.740
Sadly, she was so brainwashed by Rasputin,
that she refused to believe the governess.

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:56.010
The girls were growing older- the eldest daughters
were already teenagers, and Anastasia was

00:04:56.010 --> 00:04:57.010
9 years old.

00:04:57.010 --> 00:04:59.870
Tyutcheva knew of Rasputin’s reputation,
and she believed Vishnyakova’s story about

00:04:59.870 --> 00:05:00.870
the rape.

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:05.210
Their parents were far too preoccupied with
their own issues, so this nanny tried to keep

00:05:05.210 --> 00:05:07.340
Rasputin away from the children.

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:11.150
The girls were very naive, and did not see
any issue with him coming to visit them at

00:05:11.150 --> 00:05:12.150
night.

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:16.580
They called him a “friend”, and when they
told Tsarina Alexandra that the nanny was

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:20.030
trying to keep him out of the nursery at night,
she was fired.

00:05:20.030 --> 00:05:24.340
According to the children, Rasputin was simply
visiting to tell them bedtime stories and

00:05:24.340 --> 00:05:26.160
help them say their prayers.

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:29.340
Back in his hometown, Rasputin actually did
have children of his own.

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:33.970
So it’s always possible that he was acting
on fatherly instinct, but his reputation of

00:05:33.970 --> 00:05:37.490
a sexual deviant would be enough for anyone
to be worried.

00:05:37.490 --> 00:05:41.660
After Tyutcheva was fired, she told everyone
she knew about the story.

00:05:41.660 --> 00:05:45.590
This sparked a rumor that Rasputin had free
reign to visit the princesses at night- in

00:05:45.590 --> 00:05:46.850
a sexual way.

00:05:46.850 --> 00:05:50.930
There is no evidence that Rasputin ever actually
abused the Romanov children- but, of course,

00:05:50.930 --> 00:05:54.340
we will never really know what went on behind
closed doors.

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:59.230
However, it was around this time that Tsar
Nicholas ordered Rasputin to leave St. Petersburg.

00:05:59.230 --> 00:06:02.590
When World War I first began, Anastasia was
only 13 years old.

00:06:02.590 --> 00:06:06.670
Her mother and sisters were old enough to
volunteer as nurses for The Red Cross.

00:06:06.670 --> 00:06:11.240
She did her duty by playing pool and chess
with the soldiers, and entertained people

00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:13.240
with her wild energy and laughter.

00:06:13.240 --> 00:06:17.030
As a teenager, Anastasia used her acting talents
for good.

00:06:17.030 --> 00:06:21.110
What seemed like mean-spirited pranks she
played in her childhood actually ended up

00:06:21.110 --> 00:06:22.660
being a love for comedy.

00:06:22.660 --> 00:06:27.020
Her family and members of the staff would
often put on their own plays and performances.

00:06:27.020 --> 00:06:31.550
According to witnesses, Anastasia was naturally
hilarious, and had everyone laughing so hard,

00:06:31.550 --> 00:06:32.949
they had tears in their eyes.

00:06:32.949 --> 00:06:37.080
Sometimes, she would bust out funny faces
when the camera was trying to capture a candid

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:39.139
moment of her and her sisters.

00:06:39.139 --> 00:06:44.180
In every difficult situation, Anastasia was
always the one trying to fix it, and make

00:06:44.180 --> 00:06:46.020
everyone around her smile.

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:49.710
After the war, Russia was suffering a terrible
depression.

00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:50.889
People were starving.

00:06:50.889 --> 00:06:54.520
Men had died during the war, and people desperately
needed help.

00:06:54.520 --> 00:06:58.169
Many people viewed the royal family as part
of the problem.

00:06:58.169 --> 00:07:02.540
Their reputation for bad decision-making with
Rasputin didn’t help, either.

00:07:02.540 --> 00:07:05.260
It was time for Communism to take hold.

00:07:05.260 --> 00:07:09.320
By the time Anastasia turned 16, the Romanov
Family was placed under house arrest by the

00:07:09.320 --> 00:07:16.880
Bolsheviks.

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:19.270
The Last Days Of The Romonovs
The Romanov family were ordered to leave their

00:07:19.270 --> 00:07:21.199
castle, and were put under house arrest.

00:07:21.199 --> 00:07:25.639
They were moved from place to place, and their
last residence was at a place called Ipatiev

00:07:25.639 --> 00:07:26.639
House.

00:07:26.639 --> 00:07:30.729
Even in these dark moments, Anastasia was
still trying to make everyone laugh and smile.

00:07:30.729 --> 00:07:34.560
It was noted that she adjusted the best to
their new life in captivity.

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:38.789
One of the guards told a story of Anastasia
trying to open a window for some fresh air,

00:07:38.789 --> 00:07:41.920
and they shot at her, to stop her from trying
to escape.

00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:46.620
She turned to the guard and stuck her tongue
out, slammed the window shut, and walked away.

00:07:46.620 --> 00:07:49.060
Even in this awful situation, she was fearless.

00:07:49.060 --> 00:07:53.640
At first, Lenin wrote that the family was
in captivity, and promised that they were

00:07:53.640 --> 00:07:54.640
still alive.

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:58.490
However, he soon realized that as long as
the royal bloodline still existed, there would

00:07:58.490 --> 00:08:02.669
always be a chance that soldiers loyal to
the Romonovs would try to overthrow him.

00:08:02.669 --> 00:08:07.930
He decided that the only way for Communism
to exist was to execute the entire family.

00:08:07.930 --> 00:08:11.720
The family was made to walk down to a basement,
where a group of men were ordered to shoot

00:08:11.720 --> 00:08:12.720
at them.

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:15.961
During the execution, they gunmen realized
that the daughters were still alive, even

00:08:15.961 --> 00:08:18.820
after being shot multiple times in the chest.

00:08:18.820 --> 00:08:22.569
It turns out that they were all wearing corsets
that had been lined with the royal gem stones,

00:08:22.569 --> 00:08:25.560
and this acted like a bulletproof vest.

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:29.900
After this, each of the family members received
a shot to the head to ensure that they actually

00:08:29.900 --> 00:08:30.900
did die.

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:34.539
However, some believe that these gem-lined
corsets would have been enough for the gunmen

00:08:34.539 --> 00:08:37.659
to hesitate to kill the two youngest members
of the family, Alexei and Anastasia.

00:08:37.659 --> 00:08:41.719
At this point, all of the older daughters
were full-grown women, but Anastasia and Alexei

00:08:41.719 --> 00:08:44.149
were only 13 and 16 years old.

00:08:44.149 --> 00:08:47.709
They were innocent children, and they didn’t
deserve to die.

00:08:47.709 --> 00:08:50.180
Many hoped that the guards would have taken
pity on them.

00:08:50.180 --> 00:08:54.470
One of the men in the firing squad, Rudolf
Lacher, was originally from Austria, and he

00:08:54.470 --> 00:08:56.650
was staying in Russia as a prisoner of war.

00:08:56.650 --> 00:09:01.370
As a foreigner, he had no loyalty to the imperial
family, and was ordered to serve the Bolsheviks.

00:09:01.370 --> 00:09:04.590
He claimed that he pulled over the truck when
it got stuck in the mud.

00:09:04.590 --> 00:09:07.970
He could hear a rustling in the back, and
when he went to check on the bodies, he discovered

00:09:07.970 --> 00:09:11.060
that Anastasia was still alive, and he helped
her escape.

00:09:11.060 --> 00:09:15.570
However, there were other quotes from Lacher
that indicated the total opposite was true.

00:09:15.570 --> 00:09:19.050
He was once quoted saying that he didn’t
care about killing the Romanov family.

00:09:19.050 --> 00:09:22.300
They weren’t relatives of his, and he was
doing his duty for the Bolsheviks.

00:09:22.300 --> 00:09:27.400
But this confession was enough to spark the
rumors that Anastasia had actually escaped.

00:09:27.400 --> 00:09:35.360
Anna Anderson
In 1920, a young woman in Berlin, Germany

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:38.180
attempted to jump off a bridge to commit suicide.

00:09:38.180 --> 00:09:42.130
Nearby police officers saved her life, and
brought her to a mental hospital for treatment.

00:09:42.130 --> 00:09:45.170
She was beautiful, and spoke with a thick
Russian accent.

00:09:45.170 --> 00:09:48.480
When the doctors asked her who she was, she
replied that she didn’t know.

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:53.170
There were scars all over her body, including
a triangle-shaped indentation in her foot,

00:09:53.170 --> 00:09:55.380
and deep lacerations on her back.

00:09:55.380 --> 00:10:00.810
It was clear that she had survived some sort
of horrific trauma, and her brain had shut

00:10:00.810 --> 00:10:01.810
down.

00:10:01.810 --> 00:10:04.000
Every night, she would wake up screaming to
her nightmares.

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:09.960
The memories were too painful for her to recall
when she was awake.

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:12.780
The doctors gave her the nickname “Miss
Unknown.”

00:10:12.780 --> 00:10:17.550
Two years later, Miss Unknown was still living
in a mental hospital in Germany, when a Russian

00:10:17.550 --> 00:10:19.690
woman was admitted as a patient.

00:10:19.690 --> 00:10:23.870
The woman was shocked, and immediately kneeled
in front of the young woman.

00:10:23.870 --> 00:10:27.000
She said that this was the missing Princess
Anastasia.

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:29.730
Miss Unknown didn’t confirm or deny this
statement.

00:10:29.730 --> 00:10:34.600
She had no idea who she actually was, and
becoming a princess sounded just fine.

00:10:34.600 --> 00:10:37.220
To the doctors, this actually made a lot of
sense.

00:10:37.220 --> 00:10:41.769
Miss Unknown had clearly endured something
horrific, and it was the answer to a mystery

00:10:41.769 --> 00:10:43.610
they had long been trying to solve.

00:10:43.610 --> 00:10:47.660
Comparing photos of both Miss Unknown and
Anastasia next to one another, it is easy

00:10:47.660 --> 00:10:49.959
to see why so many people believed this to
be true.

00:10:49.959 --> 00:10:52.670
They have the same ears, nose, and eyes.

00:10:52.670 --> 00:10:54.709
Miss Unknown was also the same age.

00:10:54.709 --> 00:10:59.410
In 1922, newspapers began to claim that the
princess had been found, and her photo was

00:10:59.410 --> 00:11:00.410
published.

00:11:00.410 --> 00:11:03.420
Friends and family of the Romanovs came from
all over the world to visit her.

00:11:03.420 --> 00:11:07.740
They would ask her questions that only Anastasia
would know, and yet somehow, this woman knew

00:11:07.740 --> 00:11:08.990
many of the answers.

00:11:08.990 --> 00:11:11.570
The opinion of the friends and family was
divided 50/50.

00:11:11.570 --> 00:11:16.130
While her case was under investigation, she
was invited to stay in castles with Romanov

00:11:16.130 --> 00:11:20.180
cousins, and several people sent her money
to help pay for her expenses.

00:11:20.180 --> 00:11:24.550
Anastasia’s tutor, Sydney Gibbes, was one
of the most outspoken critics.

00:11:24.550 --> 00:11:27.160
He vividly remembered his student’s personality.

00:11:27.160 --> 00:11:31.830
No matter how much they looked the same, this
woman lacked Anastasia’s wit and sense of

00:11:31.830 --> 00:11:32.830
humor.

00:11:32.830 --> 00:11:36.279
Another tutor, Pierre Guillard, agreed that
they found the true princess, at first.

00:11:36.279 --> 00:11:39.870
However, once he spent more time around this
woman, he agreed that they were not the same

00:11:39.870 --> 00:11:40.870
person.

00:11:40.870 --> 00:11:42.640
She was soft-spoken and lady-like.

00:11:42.640 --> 00:11:47.170
For relatives who only saw her when she was
on her best behavior, it was easier to imagine

00:11:47.170 --> 00:11:48.260
that she could be Anastasia.

00:11:48.260 --> 00:11:53.350
But for people who knew what she was really
like, this was an entirely different person.

00:11:53.350 --> 00:11:57.170
The surviving members of the royal family
met up to discuss whether or not this woman

00:11:57.170 --> 00:12:00.529
deserved to receive the inheritance of the
Romanov family.

00:12:00.529 --> 00:12:04.269
In the end, they came to a vote, and decided
that there was not enough evidence to prove

00:12:04.269 --> 00:12:06.110
that she was the real Anastasia.

00:12:06.110 --> 00:12:11.089
Her supporters were shocked, because they
felt that she was clearly the true princess.

00:12:11.089 --> 00:12:16.410
Grand Duchess Xenia Alexandrovna, sister of
Tsar Nicholas II and Anastasia’s aunt, wrote

00:12:16.410 --> 00:12:20.750
that she was outraged by this decision, and
that the remaining family was just greedy.

00:12:20.750 --> 00:12:25.360
For the friends and family who believed her,
they felt very sorry for this disowned princess,

00:12:25.360 --> 00:12:27.769
and wanted to whatever they could to help
her.

00:12:27.769 --> 00:12:31.760
She lived a life of luxury, and she was given
anything she would ever want.

00:12:31.760 --> 00:12:35.940
She eventually moved to New York with a woman
named Annie Burr Jennings, where she was able

00:12:35.940 --> 00:12:37.930
to live in a gorgeous penthouse.

00:12:37.930 --> 00:12:39.810
She changed her name to Anna Anderson.

00:12:39.810 --> 00:12:43.100
She met a man named Jack Manahan, who was
a history teacher in Virginia.

00:12:43.100 --> 00:12:46.459
They lived out a very happy life together
in the United States.

00:12:46.459 --> 00:12:52.410
Ms. Anderson went to her grave claiming that
she was the Grand Duchess Anastasia.

00:12:52.410 --> 00:12:59.839
DNA Evidence
After the fall of the Soviet Union in 1991,

00:12:59.839 --> 00:13:02.700
historians searched for the bodies of the
Romanov family.

00:13:02.700 --> 00:13:06.589
After confirming their identities through
DNA tests with surviving relatives, they were

00:13:06.589 --> 00:13:11.630
given a proper burial at the St. Peter and
Paul Cathedral in St. Petersburg, and canonized

00:13:11.630 --> 00:13:12.630
as saints in 1998.

00:13:12.630 --> 00:13:16.769
However, the bodies of Alexei and Anastasia
were missing.

00:13:16.769 --> 00:13:21.519
This was enough to fuel the fire of the debate.

00:13:21.519 --> 00:13:26.329
Was Anna Anderson actually Anastasia, after
all?

00:13:26.329 --> 00:13:31.290
This inspired several movies and plays, including
the 1997 animated movie Anastasia.

00:13:31.290 --> 00:13:36.470
In 2007, the bones of two human bodies were
found buried in the middle of the woods Siberia.

00:13:36.470 --> 00:13:38.959
One was a male, and the other was female.

00:13:38.959 --> 00:13:43.430
The bones showed signs of being doused with
acid and burned by fire, as well.

00:13:43.430 --> 00:13:48.560
It was finally confirmed through DNA testing
that these were the missing bodies of Anastasia

00:13:48.560 --> 00:13:49.560
and Alexei.

00:13:49.560 --> 00:13:53.230
According to records and forensic evidence,
scientists and historians now believe that

00:13:53.230 --> 00:13:57.120
the men who killed the Romanovs attempted
to burn the bodies, but started off with the

00:13:57.120 --> 00:13:59.880
two smallest- Alexi and Anastasia.

00:13:59.880 --> 00:14:03.149
They covered the bones in sulfuric acid, and
then set them on fire.

00:14:03.149 --> 00:14:06.370
When they discovered that this did not completely
destroy the evidence, they decided to move

00:14:06.370 --> 00:14:08.899
along and bury the rest of the bodies elsewhere.

00:14:08.899 --> 00:14:13.140
This is why the two children had been separated
from the rest of their family.

00:14:13.140 --> 00:14:17.080
As for Anna Anderson, she was dead years before
the truth of her identity was revealed.

00:14:17.080 --> 00:14:19.360
It turns out that she was a Polish factory
worker.

00:14:19.360 --> 00:14:21.500
She was married, and worked together with
her husband.

00:14:21.500 --> 00:14:25.089
One day, a grenade was tossed into the factory,
and exploded.

00:14:25.089 --> 00:14:29.760
She had to witness the love of her life and
several co-workers dying around her, before

00:14:29.760 --> 00:14:31.360
receiving a serious head injury.

00:14:31.360 --> 00:14:34.630
Her body was scarred from the shrapnel of
the explosion.

00:14:34.630 --> 00:14:38.350
After recovering from her wounds, she had
no family or friends to help her home.

00:14:38.350 --> 00:14:43.410
She somehow made her way to Germany, which
is where the police found her, and the rest

00:14:43.410 --> 00:14:44.440
is history.

00:14:44.440 --> 00:14:47.790
After revealing the truth of her identity,
many people began to call Anna Anderson a

00:14:47.790 --> 00:14:48.889
“fraud” and a liar.

00:14:48.889 --> 00:14:54.140
Since she was able to live a life of luxury,
many consider her to be a skilled con-woman.

00:14:54.140 --> 00:14:58.000
However, it’s very possible that in her
mental state, she may have actually believed

00:14:58.000 --> 00:15:00.060
she really was Anastasia.

00:15:00.060 --> 00:15:03.480
Before she was discovered in the mental hospital,
she was a young woman who had nothing left

00:15:03.480 --> 00:15:04.480
to live for.

00:15:04.480 --> 00:15:06.410
She was ready to take her own life.

00:15:06.410 --> 00:15:11.680
But becoming Anastasia gave her an identity,
a purpose, and a family who loved her.

00:15:11.680 --> 00:15:15.839
For the people who desperately needed to regain
their faith in humanity, they needed to believe

00:15:15.839 --> 00:15:18.740
the innocent children had survived the slaughter.

00:15:18.740 --> 00:15:22.930
In her own way, Anna Anderson gave them a
reason to believe in miracles.

00:15:22.930 --> 00:15:27.110
From what we know about the real Anastasia,
she lived to make people smile.

00:15:27.110 --> 00:15:31.790
If she knew that her memory and even the mere
possibility of her being alive brought so

00:15:31.790 --> 00:16:03.790
much hope and joy to so many people, she would
surely be proud.

